---
alwaysApply: true
description: Keep Node builtins out of browser bundles; typecheck, lint and build all pass when this is violated.
globs:
name: coding-client-node-boundary
---

Never let a Node builtin become reachable from browser code.

A `node:*` import anywhere on a client module's import graph ships a Vite stub to the
browser — a Proxy that throws on first property access:

```
Uncaught Error: Module "node:os" has been externalized for browser compatibility.
Cannot access "node:os.homedir" in client code.
```

## Why this needs a written rule

**`typecheck`, `lint` and `build` all pass when you violate it.** Vite externalizes Node
builtins for the browser rather than erroring, so nothing fails until a user loads a page.
The `check-client-boundary` Nx target (`scripts/check-client-node-boundary.ts`) is the only
thing standing between a bad import and a user-facing runtime error. It runs in CI on the
four React Router apps and reads their `build/client` output, so it catches transitive
reachability, not just direct imports.

## A package's name is not a signal

`@openthrottle/nodejs-utils` sounds server-only and is imported by browser code — which is
exactly how this broke. Adding `expandHome` (one `node:os` import) to that package's barrel
reached all eight client modules that import `isRecord` from it.

**Barrels are the amplifier.** One `node:*` import anywhere behind a package's `index.ts`
reaches every client module importing anything from that package.

## The three escape hatches

```ts
// GOOD — the code belongs on the server. This is how the original break was fixed:
// expandHome moved to applications/openthrottle-server/src/services/paths/.

// GOOD — type-only, erased at compile time.
import type { WorkspaceConfig } from '@openthrottle/openthrottle-ide';

// GOOD — behind a *.server.ts module, which never enters the client bundle.
const { runIdeEngine } = await import('./ide-engine.server.ts');
```

```ts
// BAD — in a package that browser code imports, however indirectly.
import { homedir } from 'node:os';
```

Node-heavy packages (`openthrottle-agentic-utils`, `openthrottle-ide`,
`openthrottle-skills`, `openthrottle-drivers`) are fine as they are: they are reached only
through `*.server.ts` and `import type`. The rule is not about which package the code lives
in — it is about what a client module can transitively reach.
